The one-step proof is difficult because the three detectors’ `signalPrev` registers are separate state variables. The needed strengthening is that they hold the same sampled input.

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (red.signalPrev == fed.signalPrev) && (fed.signalPrev == bed.signalPrev); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Reset sets all three history registers to zero, and every subsequent clock copies the same signal into each, so the equality is inductive. Given this equality, the next rising and falling outputs are the two mutually exclusive terms of the XOR computed by the both-edge detector. Thus a rising or falling output implies both_edges in the next state, making the strengthened property 1-inductive."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

The base case requires a sampled reset, or equivalent initialization; without it, the output registers are unconstrained initially.

The difficult step is relating three separately stored edge outputs. The useful strengthening is to show that their delayed-input registers always agree. Assuming the formal run includes a clocked reset, each equality is 1-inductive: reset sets both registers to zero, and every other clock loads both from `signal`. Together, they make the target property 1-inductive because the next rising-or-falling output equals the next both-edge output. `disable iff (rst)` suppresses assertion checking during reset; it does not itself establish an initial reset state. ([accellera.org](https://www.accellera.org/images/resources/videos/SystemVerilog_Assertions_Tutorial_2016.pdf?utm_source=openai))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (red.signalPrev == bed.signalPrev); endproperty",
      "explanation": "The rising-edge and both-edge detectors reset their history bits to zero and subsequently capture the same signal. This lets the proof express the next rising-edge output using the both-edge detector's history."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (fed.signalPrev == bed.signalPrev); endproperty",
      "explanation": "The falling-edge and both-edge detectors likewise maintain identical history bits. With both lemmas, the next outputs satisfy (signal && !prev) || (!signal && prev) == (signal ^ prev), proving the target's one-step transition."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

The two detectors’ history registers must agree with the both-edge detector’s history register. With that invariant, a rising or falling transition necessarily produces the XOR transition. **Assume reset is clocked before the property is checked**: `disable iff (rst)` suppresses an assertion check but does not initialize state that has never been reset. ([accellera.org](https://www.accellera.org/images/eda/sv-ec/att-4694/default_disable_1648.070904_dk.pdf?utm_source=openai))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (red.signalPrev == bed.signalPrev); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Reset makes both histories zero; every subsequent clock loads the same signal into them. This lets a rising-edge output use the both-edge detector's history."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (fed.signalPrev == bed.signalPrev); endproperty",
      "explanation": "The falling-edge and both-edge histories likewise remain equal. Together, the lemmas give a common prior value p: the next outputs are signal && !p, !signal && p, and signal ^ p, making the target implication inductive."
    }
  ]
}
</json>
